---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import DataSizeTool from '../../components/tools/DataSizeTool.astro';
import { toolBySlug } from '../../data/tools';
import { convertSize, formatSize, unitLabel } from '../../lib/data-size';

const tool = toolBySlug('data-size-converter');

// Every fact and error message below comes from the real engine at build time.
const decimalSample = convertSize(1, 'TB', 'decimal');
const binarySample = convertSize(1, 'TB', 'binary');
if (!decimalSample.ok || !binarySample.ok) throw new Error('the worked examples must succeed');

const driveExample = decimalSample;
const driveActualBinaryGB = convertSize(driveExample.value.bytes, 'B', 'binary');
if (!driveActualBinaryGB.ok) throw new Error('the drive example conversion must succeed');

const mbpsExample = convertSize(100, 'MB', 'decimal');
if (!mbpsExample.ok) throw new Error('the Mbps example must succeed');

const negativeErr = convertSize(-1, 'MB', 'decimal');
if (negativeErr.ok) throw new Error('page claims negative sizes are refused');

const nonNumericErr = convertSize(Number('abc'), 'MB', 'decimal');
if (nonNumericErr.ok) throw new Error('page claims non-numeric input is refused');

const steps = [
  'Type a size and choose which <strong>From unit</strong> it\'s in.',
  'Pick <strong>Decimal (1000)</strong> or <strong>Binary (1024)</strong> (see the FAQ if you\'re not sure which one you need).',
  'Every unit updates at once: bytes through petabytes, plus bits.',
  'Click any result to copy just that value.',
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Why does my 1 TB hard drive show as only 931 GB in Windows?',
    a: `Manufacturers advertise storage in decimal terabytes (1 TB = 1,000,000,000,000 bytes, the "Decimal" mode here), but Windows reports free space in binary gibibytes while still calling them "GB" (1 GiB = 1,073,741,824 bytes). The same ${driveExample.value.bytes.toLocaleString()} bytes reads as <strong>${formatSize(driveExample.value.values.GB)} GB</strong> in decimal but only <strong>${formatSize(driveActualBinaryGB.value.values.GB)} ${unitLabel('GB', 'binary')}</strong> in binary: nothing is missing, it's the same number of bytes measured against two different definitions of "giga".`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Which one should I use, decimal or binary?',
    a: 'Decimal (1000-based) is what storage manufacturers, network speeds and most marketing use. Binary (1024-based) is what operating systems, RAM and file managers usually display, and the technically correct meaning of KiB/MiB/GiB. If a number you were given doesn\'t match what this tool shows, try the other base before assuming either is wrong.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between MB and Mb (or MB/s and Mbps)?',
    a: `Capital B is bytes, lowercase b is bits. There are 8 bits in a byte. An internet plan advertised as "100 Mbps" (100 megabits per second) downloads at about ${formatSize(mbpsExample.value.bytes / 8)} bytes per second, roughly <strong>${formatSize(mbpsExample.value.values.MB / 8)} MB/s</strong>, not 100 MB/s, which is 8 times faster. This tool always shows both bytes and bits side by side for exactly this mix-up.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Does this handle fractional sizes, like 2.5 GB?',
    a: 'Yes. Any decimal number is accepted, not just whole numbers. Negative sizes and non-numeric text are refused, since neither means anything as a data size.',
  },
];
---

<ToolLayout {tool} {steps} {faq}>
  <DataSizeTool />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>Two different "gigabytes", both correct</h2>
    <p>
      A gigabyte means two different numbers of bytes depending who's counting: storage
      manufacturers, network speeds and most marketing use <strong>decimal</strong> (1 GB =
      1000<sup>3</sup> bytes, matching the metric prefix "giga"), while operating systems, RAM
      capacity and file managers usually mean <strong>binary</strong> (1 GiB = 1024<sup>3</sup>
      bytes, the correct unit is GiB, though "GB" gets used for it constantly). Neither is wrong;
      picking the wrong one for the number you already have is what produces a mismatch. This tool
      keeps the two separate instead of quietly picking one.
    </p>

    <h2>Example</h2>
    <p>1 TB (decimal) is:</p>
    <pre><code>{decimalSample.value.bytes.toLocaleString()} bytes
{formatSize(decimalSample.value.values.GB)} GB
{formatSize(decimalSample.value.bits)} bits</code></pre>
    <p>The same number of bytes read as binary (TiB) instead:</p>
    <pre><code>{formatSize(binarySample.value.values.GB)} {unitLabel('GB', 'binary')} (1 TiB = 1024 GiB, so 1 decimal TB is slightly less than 1 binary TiB)</code></pre>

    <h2>Options explained</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>From unit</strong>: Bytes, KB, MB, GB, TB or PB. Selecting a unit shows every other unit's equivalent value; the row for the unit you entered is highlighted.</li>
      <li><strong>Decimal (1000) / Binary (1024)</strong> chooses the base; see the FAQ for which one you likely want. Switching this option re-labels KB/MB/GB… as KiB/MiB/GiB… for binary, since those are the unit's real names.</li>
      <li>Click any result row to copy just that value.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Edge cases and common errors</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Negative sizes are refused.</strong> "{negativeErr.ok ? '' : negativeErr.error}": a negative amount of data doesn't mean anything here, so it's rejected rather than silently made positive.</li>
      <li><strong>Non-numeric input is refused</strong> with "{nonNumericErr.ok ? '' : nonNumericErr.error}" rather than converting as if it were zero.</li>
      <li><strong>Astronomically large sizes are refused</strong> once the byte count would exceed what a JavaScript number can represent exactly (about 9 quadrillion), rather than silently returning an imprecise answer.</li>
      <li><strong>Bits vs bytes</strong>, covered in the FAQ, is the single most common mix-up this tool exists to prevent.</li>
      <li><strong>Zero is a valid size</strong> and converts to zero in every unit, not an error.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Do the same thing in code</h2>
    <pre><code>{`// JavaScript: decimal
const gb = bytes / 1000 ** 3;
// JavaScript: binary (the correct unit is GiB, not GB)
const gib = bytes / 1024 ** 3;

# Python: same distinction
gb = bytes / 1000 ** 3
gib = bytes / 1024 ** 3`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Working with a raw timestamp or date instead of a size? The <a href="/tools/unix-timestamp-converter">Unix Timestamp Converter</a>
      and <a href="/tools/date-difference-calculator">Date Difference Calculator</a> handle those the same "show every honest interpretation" way.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
